<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  polym2: bound ClickHouse's memory on a shared host.

  Mounted into /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/ by docker-compose.infra.polym2.yml.
  Only meaningful for our private collection run; upstream's deployment has the
  box to itself and needs none of this.

  Background: on 2026-08-05 the kernel OOM-killed clickhouse-server 17 times in
  75 minutes. The host has 14 GB and runs a dozen unrelated services plus a KVM
  guest, so ClickHouse had roughly 4.6 GB to work with and was reaching 5.4 GB
  under merge load. The kill was `global_oom` rather than a cgroup limit, meaning
  the kernel was picking a victim host-wide and could as easily have taken
  something else down.

  Two defences, and the order matters:

    1. `max_server_memory_usage` below makes ClickHouse refuse work it cannot
       afford, returning MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED instead of growing into the OOM
       killer. A refused insert is a visible error in the sink's [DATA-GAP] log;
       an OOM kill is a silent restart. Both lose data, only one tells you.
    2. `mem_limit` in the compose overlay is the backstop, one step above this,
       so a miss here is contained to this container rather than the host.

  The real reduction in merge memory is not here at all: it is TTL_MINUTES going
  from 2880 to 360 in docker-compose.polym2.yml, which takes the table from ~6.3
  billion rows to ~780 million. This file only stops a bad hour from taking the
  host with it.

  Deliberately nothing else. An earlier version also set `background_pool_size`
  and `max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool`, which was speculative tuning and
  did not survive contact: MergeTree cross-checks those against each other, and
  a pool of 8 is smaller than the default
  `number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation` of 20, so the server
  refused to start. Tune the pool only with the whole set of related defaults in
  hand.

  Careful with comments in this file: XML forbids a double hyphen inside one, and
  ClickHouse refuses to start on a malformed config rather than skipping it,
  which crash-loops the container.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!-- 4.5 GiB, sitting below the 5 GB cgroup limit in the compose overlay so
         ClickHouse hits its own accounting first and can fail gracefully.

         Started at 3.5 GiB, which held the database up but starved the hourly
         export: a full hour is ~153M rows and the export competes with live
         inserts and merges rather than running on a quiet server, so it failed
         at every row-group size tried. See clickhouse-users.d/polym2-parquet.xml
         for those measurements.

         Not raised further because the host has 14 GB and the rest of it is
         spoken for: the three collector services measure ~4.7 GB together and
         the unrelated containers another ~2.5 GB. -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>4831838208</max_server_memory_usage>
</clickhouse>
